  "worked_solution": "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all natural numbers greater than or equal to $3$ but less than $9.$ So,\n\n$$\nA={3,4,5,6,7,8}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers which satisfy the inequality $3x<10.$ So,\n\n$$\nB={1,2,3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A\u2286B$ is false. For example, $4\\in A$ but $4\\notin B.$\n\n- $B\u2286A$ is false. For example, $1\\in B$ but $1\\notin A.$\n\n- $\u2205\u2286A$ is true. The null set $\u2205={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
